      <p class="panel-note">Why does this matter? Red blood cells carry antigens, protein flags on their surface, and your plasma carries antibodies against foreign antigens. If you receive blood your immune system doesn't recognise as "self," it triggers a haemolytic transfusion reaction: the antibodies attack the donated cells, causing them to burst. This can be fatal. The ABO system and the Rhesus factor (the + / − ) are the two most clinically critical of over 30 known blood group systems.</p>
